Pinned input files for the sieve/strategies tests
=================================================

Both files below are in the format that survey_cofactoring_methods and
bench_cofactoring_methods write and that build_cofactoring_strategies reads:
one line per factoring method,

    method B1 B2 | p proba_p proba_(p+1) ... | c1 c2 c3 c4 |

(see sieve/strategies/README for the meaning of the columns). A line
starting with '#' is a comment, in this format and in the two others that
this directory reads -- the factorization patterns and the strategies --
so the references under expected/ label each of their sections with one
and remain valid input for the reader that produced them.

They are pinned rather than produced on the fly because
bench_cofactoring_methods measures wall-clock time, so the
survey_cofactoring_methods/bench_cofactoring_methods chain does not give the
same output twice even at a fixed -seed.

Each of the two carries its own provenance in a comment header: what it is,
how it was produced, and what to beware of when reading it. That is where to
look, and where to keep it up to date -- not here.

sample_methods.txt is hand-written and covers the six method families;
data_fm_25.txt is cado's own default strategy benched for fbb = 25.


expected/ -- the pinned outputs
===============================

test_strategies_pinned.sh and test_strategies_pinned_notime.sh compare what
the binaries produce against the references in expected/. Each reference
begins with a comment naming the command that produced it, written by the
script from the same place that runs that command, so the two cannot drift
apart; there is deliberately no list of them here. Both scripts take REGEN=1
to rewrite the references in place, which is how they were made and how they
should be remade after a deliberate change of behaviour -- read the diff
before committing it.

The instance is small enough that the whole thing runs in well under a
second. One thing about it is worth stating here because no single file
shows it: fbb = ceil(log2(lim+1)) = 11 for the lim used, so a cofactor of
fewer than 2*fbb-1 = 21 bits is taken to be prime and gets a trivial
strategy. The band [21, 26] is where the generator does work, and it is
where the references are taken.

Two things are deliberately not pinned:

  - survey_cofactoring_methods -ch. The convex hull ranks methods by
    (probability, time), so timing noise changes which methods survive:
    eight runs at one seed gave three different answers, with hulls of two
    different sizes. It is smoke-checked only, and the same hull code is
    pinned through -fch, which reads a fixed file.

  - the timing columns of anything survey_cofactoring_methods or
    bench_cofactoring_methods writes. Those are wall-clock measurements and
    never come out the same twice; the references with a .notime suffix have
    them stripped before the comparison.

Where these references are valid
================================

They were produced on x86-64 with gcc, and the two scripts only run where
that arithmetic is reproducible. CMake guards them; if they do not appear
in the ctest list, this is why.

  - Byte-exact pinning needs a double to be a double. On i386 with x87
    arithmetic, which is what gcc selects for -m32 unless told
    -mfpmath=sse, intermediates keep 80 bits, and a decision taken on two
    quantities that agree to the last bit goes the other way.
    choose_cofactoring_strategies bisects for the slope at which to read
    the trade-off frontier, stopping at 1e-4; where two strategies are
    near-tied at that slope, the two builds pick different ones. They are
    just as good, but they are not the same ones, and since what differs
    is a choice and not a digit, no tolerance in the comparison would
    absorb it. config/excess-precision.cpp is the test, via
    FLT_EVAL_METHOD.

    Everything before that step -- the patterns, the one-sided
    strategies, the merge, the whole matrix and its digest -- does agree
    between the two builds.

  - The .notime references need a 64-bit unsigned long as well.
    survey_cofactoring_methods draws the sizes of its random composites
    through random_uniform(), which consumes ULONG_MAX worth of randomness
    per call, so a fixed -seed does not give the same stream on a 32-bit
    build, and the methods surveyed differ. That is a property of
    random_uniform() in utils, not of this directory.

Both are checks on the reference, not on the code: a 32-bit build computes
strategies that are just as good, and everything else in this directory
passes there.
